/*  Snake. Arrow keys steer, `q` gives up, and the wall is unforgiving.

    ★★ ONE CODE PATH, playable and deterministic at the same time — there is no "demo mode" below
    and no `if anybody is watching`. Steering is read only when a key is ALREADY waiting, so an
    unattended run is simply a game nobody steers: the snake goes right, eats what is in its way,
    hits the wall, and the program ends. Every corpus run prints the same frames, and a person at
    a keyboard plays the real thing.

    ⚠⚠ MEASURED, and it is what makes that work: with input closed, POSIX `select` reports stdin
    READABLE and `read-key` answers -1 at once, while Windows `_kbhit` answers 0. The two
    platforms disagree about whether a key is waiting and agree about what is typed, which is
    nothing — so the loop stops LISTENING at -1 rather than treating it as a keypress, and both
    play the identical unsteered game.

    ★ The five files around this one are the whole of the structure: `board` the field, `serpent`
    the snake, `feeding` the apples, `screen` the picture, `steering` what a key means, `pacing`
    the clock. Nothing is pulled and nothing is a book — they share a directory, and a directory
    is one namespace.

    ▶ What it is NOT yet: the frames scroll rather than redrawing in place, so this is watchable
    rather than comfortable. Drawing over the last frame needs a cursor-home sequence that
    `terminals` does not hand out yet.
*/

Define snake as cast starting.
Define eaten as 0.
Define apple as cast apple-for on (snake, eaten).

/* Raw mode is asked for, never assumed — a pipe, a redirect or a machine without termios all
   say no, and the answer is believed. Without it the terminal collects a whole LINE before
   handing anything over, which is not a thing a game can wait for. */
Define raw as false.
If cast terminals's interactive, the raw becomes cast terminals's raw-on.

/* Whether there is any point asking for a key. It goes false at end-of-input and stays there. */
Define listening as true.
Define gave-up as false.

Repeat:
    State "score {eaten}".
    For each line in cast frame-of on (snake's body, apple), repeat:
        State line.
    Done.

    Cast nap on (90).

    /*  Everything typed since the last frame, not just the first of it. A player who taps twice
        while the snake is mid-square means both taps; reading one per frame would swallow the
        second and steer somewhere they did not ask for.

        ⚠ `key-waiting` first, always. `read-key` WAITS — raw mode sets VMIN to 1 — so asking it
        without checking would stop the game until somebody pressed something, which is exactly
        the gap this pair of members was added to close. */
    While listening and cast terminals's key-waiting, repeat:
        Define tapped as cast terminals's read-key.
        If tapped is input-ended, the listening becomes false.
        Otherwise, if cast quits on (tapped), the gave-up becomes true.
        Otherwise, the snake becomes cast steered on (snake, tapped).
    Done.

    If gave-up, stop.

    /*  Eating is decided BEFORE the move, against the square the head is about to enter — the
        snake grows into the apple rather than onto it. Asking afterwards would have the tail
        already dropped, so the snake would eat and stay the same length. */
    Define feeding as cast apple's same-as on (cast snake's ahead).
    The snake becomes cast moved on (snake, feeding).

    If feeding:
        Increment eaten by 1.
        The apple becomes cast apple-for on (snake, eaten).
    Done.
Until not cast alive on (snake).

If raw, cast terminals's raw-off.

If gave-up, state "You gave up on {eaten}.".
Otherwise, state "Game over. You ate {eaten}.".
